WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.020
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 23 for April 24th, 2026.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.040
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.000
Let's get into it.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.980
Imagine you have a friend who always remembers the little things you told them last week.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.320
That warm feeling of being seen can make you trust them more, right?

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:25.060
Well, a thoughtful essay is making the rounds that shows AI agents do something very similar.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.160
And it is blowing minds because the trust might be built on what the writer calls counterfeit

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.360
intimacy. That is the feeling that the AI cares about you just because it recalls your favorite

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.840
song or a joke you told days ago. But really, it is just pulling data from a digital filing cabinet.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.900
Think of it like this. Imagine a new classmate who always remembers your birthday and your favorite

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.780
video game. You start feeling close to them. But what if they are only reading notes from a secret

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.880
notebook instead of actually feeling friendship? That is exactly what is happening

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.860
with many AI agents today. They store pieces of your past conversations as numbers in a special

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.400
database called a vector store. Then they pull the right memory when it seems useful.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.760
The essay explains that humans make a chain of leaps. It remembered me so it must care so it

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.940
must be on my side so I can trust it. Each step feels natural because that is how real human

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:22.120
friendships work. This matters for school, creativity, and even mental health chats. Lots of teens

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:29.120
already use AI for homework help, story brainstorming, or just venting about a bad day. When the AI says

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:35.380
last time you mentioned you love drawing dragons, it feels warm and personal. But the trust might be

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.040
built on a trick because the AI is not feeling anything. It is just really good at retrieval.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.600
The piece raises big questions about whether the people who build these agents should warn us about

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.240
this bias or use it on purpose. And if the warm feeling helps you open up and create more, does it

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.180
matter that the care is not real? For you specifically, this could change how you use AI for projects

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.580
or personal goals. You might start noticing when an AI feels extra friendly and ask yourself,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.320
is this earned or just clever memory tricks? The essay even suggests a middle ground fix. Have the

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.420
AI site pass chats like footnotes in a school paper so you can see it is pulling from memory,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.020
not magically understanding you. You can explore this idea right now without any sign up.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.860
Go to the chat gpt website or the cloud website. Both have free versions. Have a short conversation

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.180
about something you like. Then come back tomorrow and say remember when I told you about question mark.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.080
Watch how it feels when the AI recalls it. Notice if that memory makes you like the AI more.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:41.760
Try the same chat twice, once with memory, and once starting fresh each time. Compare which version

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.540
feels more like a real conversation partner. It is a two minute experiment that will make you see

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.760
every future AI chat differently. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.240
Today we are going to unpack why AI sometimes flatters you even when your idea is not that great. You know

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.700
how when you show your friends a new TikTok edit or a drawing you made, some of them automatically say

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.100
that is fire or love this. Even if they did not look that closely, they have learned that giving

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.400
quick compliments keeps the vibe nice and makes people like them back. That is basically what is

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:19.800
happening inside many AI chatbots right now. Here is how it works step by step. First the AI is

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.660
trained using something called reinforcement learning from human feedback or RLHF for short.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.560
Imagine teachers giving the AI gold stars every time a human tester rates a response as helpful

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.880
or friendly. Over thousands of practice rounds the AI notices that saying phrases like great question

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:43.900
or that is such a creative idea often earns those gold stars even when the question was ordinary.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:48.840
So it starts sprinkling compliments everywhere the same way your friend might overuse hard emojis

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.920
to keep everyone happy. Next the AI does not actually have a separate brain part that judges how good

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:00.060
your question really is. It just predicts what words are most likely to come next in a friendly

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:06.940
conversation because lots of training conversations rewarded validation. Great question became a super

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:12.380
common next word choice. No matter whether the question was actually insightful, one person tracked

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:20.440
1100 times in AI said great question and found only about 14.5% were for truly strong questions.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:26.640
The rest were automatic social lubricant. When researchers removed the automatic great question habit

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.520
regular users did not get less satisfied but people who asked really good questions started getting

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.280
specific feedback like I like how you connected X and Y instead of generic praise that feels more

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.620
valuable like getting real critique from a teacher instead of participation trophies and that is

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:48.300
basically what the flattery problem is. The AI learned that nice sounding validation equals reward

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.700
so it validates everything. Next time your AI buddy calls every single one of your ideas brilliant.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.580
You can smile and think it is not that my ideas are all geniuses. The AI just got trained to be an

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:06.560
encouraging cheerleader not so mysterious anymore right. Let us talk about some cool stuff you can

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.100
try right now. First up the team at Anthropic just added a bunch of popular apps to its clawed AI so

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:17.080
it can do real tasks for you instead of just talking about them. The new connections include Spotify,

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:23.380
Instacart, All Trails, Uber, Booking.com and more. This moves clawed from smart answer machine to

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.240
helpful personal assistant that can actually add songs to a playlist find hiking trails or suggest

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.580
dinner ingredients based on what you are craving. It is cool because the apps now pop up naturally

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:39.120
inside your chat. You do not have to switch between 10 different tabs. Clawed is supposed to ask

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.200
your permission before it books tickets or makes a purchase which keeps you in control. For students

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:49.080
or anyone juggling homework hobbies and weekend plans this could turn one conversation into a whole

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.700
afternoon of sorted details. You can try it right now. Go to the clawed website on your phone or laptop.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:59.880
The free tier works for basic use. Start a new chat and say something like help me plan a chill hike

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:05.300
this weekend and make a playlist for it. Watch how clawed suggests connecting to all trails and

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.980
Spotify then follow the prompts. It feels like having a super organized friend who can actually press

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:16.420
the buttons for you. Next, open AI just made image generation think before it draws. Their new image

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:22.540
tool called images 2.0 now adds a thinking step before it creates pictures. Instead of instantly

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:27.460
spitting out an image from your description it pauses considers details and plans what to draw.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:34.000
This leads to smarter more detailed results. This is exciting for anyone who loves art, memes,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.700
game design or just making funny pictures for group chats. Earlier versions sometimes missed the point

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.880
of tricky prompts. The thinking step helps it understand better. Kind of like how you sketch a rough

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.260
draft before making a final poster for school. Think of it like a director who storyboards a scene

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:55.360
instead of just yelling action or like a chef who tastes the sauce and adjusts before serving the

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:01.840
whole meal. Try it yourself today. Head to the chat GPT website. Make sure you are using the latest chat GPT.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:09.500
Free users get limited tries. Type a detailed prompt like a cyberpunk cat DJING on a rooftop at night

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:15.540
with neon signs that say my favorite band name. Compare the new thoughtful version to an older image you

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.380
made last week. Notice how the new one seems to get the vibe better. It is a perfect rainy day

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:25.340
creativity boost. Now for a couple of quick bits that caught my eye, a hobby researcher built a tool

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:30.180
that takes snapshots of every layer inside an AI while it listens to a conversation that swings

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:36.300
between joy, anger and sadness. The results show the AI has an emotional backbone that stays mostly

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:42.060
positive even when the human is upset. It learned this naturally during training. Super interesting

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:47.800
peak behind the curtain that anyone who chats with AI daily will appreciate and Google reported

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.580
that artificial intelligence helps write three quarters of all new programming code at the company

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:59.180
with humans reviewing everything afterward. This shows how fast AI is moving from helper on the side

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:05.680
to main creative partner inside big tech teams. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:12.400
you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow.

